<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modena</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mutina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Modena is a city in Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-aemilia">Via Emilia</a>. Roman Mutina, it became a commune in the 12th century and from 1288 belonged mostly to the Este, who made it their capital after losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> in 1598. It was the seat of the Duchy of Modena and Reggio until 1859. Its Romanesque cathedral is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/modena.jpg" alt="Modena" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interncraq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modena_Motor_Valley_Fest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In alliance with Napoleon III of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained Lombardy; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, <b>Modena</b> and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The war of 1859 brought Lombardy by the Armistice of Villafranca and the Treaty of Zurich; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, <b>Modena</b> and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France by the Treaty of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of <b>Mutina</b>, troops loyal to Mark Antony, after initial success, were defeated on the Via Aemilia by legions of the Roman Senate under the consuls Gaius Pansa and Aulus Hirtius.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Mutina</b> in Cisalpine Gaul, Senate forces under the consuls Pansa and Hirtius, with Octavian, fought Mark Antony, who was besieging Caesar's assassin Decimus Brutus there.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It sent delegates to the congresses that united Bologna with Ferrara, <b>Modena</b> and Reggio.</blockquote><a href="/state/bolognese-republic">Bolognese Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1796 French victories ended Este rule in <b>Modena</b> and papal rule in Bologna and Ferrara.</blockquote><a href="/state/cispadane-republic">Cispadane Republic</a></li></ul></section>
